WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.610 align:start position:0%
 
British astronaut Tim Peake has  announced that he will be going back  into space for a second time delighted  to say he joins us now from Steven itch  Tim Peake good afternoon to you thanks  it's in the gift isn't it at the  European Space Agency this they will  decide when but you'll go back what on a  soyuz as you did last time how will it  work well yes the announcement was made  this morning really for my whole class  of 2009 that the extension of the  international space station program to  2024 will enable all of us to have the  opportunity for a second mission back to  the space station the timings of that  mission is yet to be determined by the  European Space Agency okay we saw you  this morning at the Science Museum  poring over the Soyuz capsule you went  up in and came back to the Kazakh steppe  on still singed from some of the  re-entry temperatures which must have  been absolutely colossal what comes  across more than anything when you look  back at those pictures of you going up  giving that wonderful thumbs up perfect  illustration of British pluck is the  sense of claustrophobia it we right in  taking that away from the sense of what  it was like did you feel terribly  claustrophobic yeah absolutely it's  something that a lot of people commented  to me about this morning when they saw  the capsule was how small it looks and  it is I mean really that's part of the  design is is when you're bringing  something back through the Earth's  atmosphere the smaller it is the easier  it is to engineer so it's a small  capsule is a very tight space and in  fact when it's packed up with a cargo  that we bring back from the space  station it's even smaller inside so  anybody who suffer from claustrophobia  certainly wouldn't do very well inside  the Soyuz I remember listening to an  interview I think it was with the late  John Glenn talking about coming back and  there was a flashing warning light  indicating that with the perhaps the  heat shield was loosed and he said he  became super sensitive to any warming in  the rear quarters did you is something I  was very surprised that I mean it sounds  strange it you know you you know that  you're going to experience very hot  temperatures when you come back through  the Earth's atmosphere and the capsule  it has to withstand 1600 degrees Celsius  but inside the capsule of course it's  very very hot and that all three of us  were absolutely drained  in sweat when we were dragged out of the  capsule drenched in sweat you couldn't  really walk as well the whole the whole  weight and the experience really took  its toll on you but do you have any idea  when you're going to be going back and  what are you going to be doing because  the last time you carried out around 200  250 experiments and you also kept  yourself entertained Carnaby do you have  any idea what you're going to be doing  going heading back to space ii darling  with the space station program the great  thing about that is that the science is  a rolling evolution and the experiments  continue to go up there what's really  exciting is since I've landed in fact  we've started DNA sequencing on board  the space station we're studying more  genetics more pharmaceutical companies  are being involved in the medical  research that we're doing so the science  program just continues to evolve and the  astronauts really whenever they go to  the space station they slot in to that  to that science program and you'll be  trained up on those experiments near at  the time so that's the job of any  astronaut is to be able to perform those  scientific experiments and also maintain  the space station which may require for  example a spacewalk or using the robotic  arm for resupply vehicles to mark my  children all their enormous families of  yours partly because you're the first  Briton to walk in space but also because  we live quite close to middle wallop the  Army Air Corps where you used to fly  from in your what Army Air Corps days I  suppose how do you put a metric how do  you measure that sense of inspiration I  mean quite clearly you know for many  people not least my children you're a  household name but what's the degree how  can you demonstrate how you have  inspired generation of young people who  perhaps might not have been as  interested in science and space as they  have been because of you I think one of  the most wonderful things for me  personally since landing has been going  on the post-flight tour having the  opportunity to visit schools  universities and have school conferences  as well and to really see the impact  that mission Principia made I know that  during the mission we reached out to  over a million school students and even  more since then and these educational  programs are continuing to run which is  incredible that's one of the great  things about it and I'm stood here at  the moment at Airbus and Stevenage in  the Mars yard where today we're opening  a stem center called discovery space and  and this is  an example of how space is continuing to  inspire a younger generation to get  involved in science and engineering and  technology and that's going to be  important not only because there are  genuinely rewarding and exciting careers  to have in that area but also we have a  skill shortage that we need to address  in the UK so trying to encourage  students at a younger age to get  involved in STEM subjects is very  important and just delighted that  Principia was able to do that and what  was recovery like when you when you  landed back on earth and your body  recovering to the toll being in space  for six months took on you your mind  your body what was that like for you and  how excited are you to spend more time  in space you're right I mean it does  take a huge toll on your body being in  space for six months but that's one of  the remarkable things one of the things  that we study is not just how quickly  the body adapts to living in space but  how quickly the body adapts to coming  back into a gravity environment and it  only took about three weeks before I  felt actually very comfortable my  balance had retained regained normality  I felt strong I felt fit enough I was  running but it actually took two it took  about six months before my muscles and  my bone density really made a decent  recovery and of course every astronaut  accepts that when you go into space  you're going to put your body through  this punishing regime but that's that's  something that we all we all sign up to  and actually the rewards of spaceflight  in terms of the science that we're  involved in in terms of view of planet  Earth and the inspirational the factor  that we can have far outweigh the  negative negative sides term status  everybody got time for one more question  I know we all to editorially ask you  about why it is you're a Martian  landscape in Stevenage but I'm going to  ignore that question because I'm really  keen to ask you instead and we I was on  presenting a year ago when you when you  had the launch and remember thinking as  everybody else must have thought  vicariously as you gave that thumbs up  in the capsule you know I think the  phrase we used at the time was that your  honor it was like going to space on a  gigantic firework that at any moment  could do something unpredictable how did  you keep yourself calm do you know that  the training is the one thing that  enables us to keep calm in those  situations we had done that launch  sequence hundreds of times in the  simulators in Star City in Russia  covering any eventualities that could  have possibly have come on the trailer  Jim well the one thing the simulator  can't give you of course the simulator  can't give you that the sounds and the  smells and the acceleration and  everything so so that's very different  inside the capsule but other than that  it's your training is is what really  keeps you calm because you're empowered  with the knowledge that if something  goes wrong at least you know what to do  you've got options and the Soyuz  spacecraft is is a great vehicle to go  into space in because it does have you  do have options at every stage during  the launch sequence if something goes  wrong well he's an all-round British  hero absolutely imperturbable he's on  thousands of meter interviews I'm sure  but he still as fresh as a daisy tim  peake thanks ever so much and good  looking to you really good good luck  thank you very much

